  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Showers and thunderstorms are expected again tonight\nand Friday. \n\nAs short waves continue to move across the Great Lakes then then turn\nsoutheast across the northern Middle Atlantic region, we will see\nmore rounds of showers and thunderstorms accompany them. We had this\nsetup last night with the showers diminishing this morning across\nPennsylvania by mid-morning. Another shot of energy is moving across\nMichigan and Lake Erie as of this afternoon and this will affect our\nareas later tonight and into Friday morning. The latest SPC outlook\nhas much of the region in a SLIGHT risk for severe weather tonight.\nDamaging winds and large hail are the primary threats with severe\nweather. \n\nIn addition, local flash flooding is possible with the thunderstorms.\nThere is limited confidence in which areas will receive any flooding,\nand also with greater storm motion, it's probable that flooding may\nbe limited. For these reasons, we have not considered any flood\nwatches attm. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot and humid conditions are forecast across \nthe region into the evening.\n\nWe'll continue the ongoing Heat Advisory for the remaining part\nof the day although in some areas we may come up a bit low of\nthe Advisory numbers. The morning clouds kept temps from rising\nearlier today, but the ongoing period of sunshine will create \nincreasingly uncomfortable readings into the evening.\n\n\n3. The Labor Day Weekend will feature mostly dry weather with at or \nbelow normal temperatures and lower humidity.\n\nBy Saturday, the trend among model guidance shows the frontal boundary\nsouth of our region, with Canadian high pressure building in across\nthe Northeast and Mid-Atlantic. This will largely end any shower\nand thunderstorm potential. Thus, Labor Day Weekend is still shaping\nup to feature nice weather, with temperatures trending at or slightly\nbelow normal and lower humidity.",
